## Build brief

**Concept — a printed clinic timetable that knows what time it is.** The clinic's real constraint is the walk-in window, so the page puts *now* inside the schedule instead of beside it.

**Product reason.** Three questions, in the order people arrive with them: can I be seen today → what will it cost and what will you ask me → how do I get there. Everything else is below that.

**Visual system.** Archivo Variable (width axis, signage scale) over Public Sans Variable — the typeface drawn for US public services; both bundled, no network font request. Colour is the triage state and nothing else: green open, amber for the last hour and after registration closes, slate closed, one restrained red reserved for 911. Full-bleed bands with hairline rules; no centred hero, no card grid.

**Signature component — the walk-in clock** (`src/WalkInClock.jsx`). The whole week on one 06:00–21:00 axis, each day a proportional bar with the last 30 minutes hatched out, today's row lit and a live sweep line crossing all seven days. It answers the visitor's actual question and could not be lifted onto another page.

**Signature behaviour.** One authored value — `nowMin` — drives everything: the sweep travels from the axis origin to the current minute while today's bar fills to the same instant, on one 0.95s transition. `data-status` on `<html>` sets `--state`, so the hero, the strip, the six service tags and the fixed call rail cannot disagree about whether you can walk in.

**Interaction baseline.** One grammar, 140ms, on every control. Verified: 16 of 17 interactive elements change on hover or focus; the 17th is the skip link, which is off-screen until focused and has a designed focus state.

**Preserved.** All seven days' hours, all six services verbatim, both assurances (hero *and* the dark promise band), address/phone/transit, the seven languages plus the interpreter line, the 911 notice, and the booking form with its validation and confirmation.

## Two decisions worth your attention

- **No photography, deliberately.** The clinic is fictional, so any sourced frontage or interior would be a fabricated claim on a page whose entire job is being trusted by people afraid of being reported. Visual material is the Lucide icon set (ISC) and drawn notation. **This is the gap:** real photographs of the frontage, the Alder Lane entrance and the front desk are the single asset that would most improve this page, and only you can supply them.
- **The 911 band is near-black with one red accent, not a red panic band** — following USWDS site-alert guidance that top-of-page position carries the urgency while heavy red produces fear rather than action.

The status is computed live, so it will read *Closed right now* outside opening hours; `?now=2026-08-31T15:20` overrides the clock so you can inspect any state.

Corrections made from the rendered pages, before/after: a 70px horizontal overflow at 390px (form grid tracks sized by the inputs' intrinsic width); the three promise headings were bottom-ragged, now on one baseline via subgrid; the call rail floated on its own gutter and now aligns with the page measure; the live-time flag collided with the axis labels; the status dot sat orphaned in the margin beside a two-line headline; all seven languages now sit on one row instead of six plus an orphan.

